            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="CokYTnKlmNwNPXlvUQEOJs" Bounds="128,12137,401,434" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Role: First applied chapter. The organizational case for Records.&#xD;&#xA;Core idea: A single spread of Records replaces multiple parallel spreads of separate values — patches become dramatically cleaner, ForEach loops become simpler, related data stays related.&#xD;&#xA;What lands:&#xD;&#xA;The before-and-after contrast: same field of shapes built with parallel spreads vs. a single spread of Records&#xD;&#xA;How ForEach simplifies when iterating over bundled data&#xD;&#xA;When bundling is the right move (and when it isn't)&#xD;&#xA;Patterns for what makes a good Record — which properties belong together&#xD;&#xA;Carrying example: A field of shapes with varied properties (position, size, color, rotation). Built first the old way with parallel spreads, then rebuilt with a Record. The reader sees the patch shrink.&#xD;&#xA;Back-references: ch. 19 (Spreads), ch. 20 (Iteration), ch. 25 (Managing Spreads)&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="I5b1GV62zAzOfFrOYpvwFU" Bounds="128,13399,991,46" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="41. Use Case II: Creating and Destroying Instances During Runtime">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">22</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="JDupOJbyIdaPXbnyJonK9D" Bounds="127,17884,406,656" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Role: The fork. Classes enter the picture — but as a specialist tool, not a co-equal alternative.&#xD;&#xA;Core idea: Records are immutable; every operation produces a new copy. Classes are mutable; they change in place. This difference shows up in the patch as solid vs dashed links — a UI tell the reader has been brushing against without knowing. Classes are powerful in specific situations, but they come with real costs that the reader has been getting for free with Records.&#xD;&#xA;What lands:&#xD;&#xA;Introduction of Class as the mutable counterpart to Record&#xD;&#xA;The State Output pin: visually disconnected on Records (new object out), visually connected on Classes (same object out, modified)&#xD;&#xA;Solid vs dashed links as the UI signature of mutability&#xD;&#xA;The side-by-side patch (the MyRecord/MyClass demonstration): same operations producing three colors on the Record side, one color on the Class side — value-vs-reference made visible in a single screen&#xD;&#xA;The decision tree, derived from the side-by-side patch rather than presented as rules: Record is the default, Class is the specialist choice with specific reasons to reach for it&#xD;&#xA;An honest acknowledgment of what switching to Class costs: Changed nodes and Cache regions only see reference changes (not mutations), Undo and SampleHold need workarounds, order of operations with parallel modifications can become unclear&#xD;&#xA;Examples: Small, dry demonstrations only — the MyRecord/MyClass side-by-side patch is the centerpiece. No carrying example, no application context. The reader sees the mechanic in isolation and reads the consequences.&#xD;&#xA;Back-references: ch. 23 (Pads — the wiring that disappears with Classes), ch. 25 (Managing Spreads — where the explicit-store pattern was first felt)&#xD;&#xA;Closing pointer: Links to TobyK's seven-part Mutability tutorial series for readers who want the deep dive into change detection, snapshots, order of operations, and the architectural implications.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="VpvBNGPxhu8QG7BR7S2k2t" Bounds="506,14497,449,434" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Role: Second applied chapter. The capability case for Records — the thing Process nodes literally cannot do.&#xD;&#xA;Core idea: Instances of a Record can be created and destroyed while the application is running. The reader builds an interactive application where Particles appear, disappear, and behave in response to user input.&#xD;&#xA;What lands:&#xD;&#xA;Creating instances dynamically: add to a spread on a trigger&#xD;&#xA;Destroying instances dynamically: remove from a spread on a trigger&#xD;&#xA;The mouse-button trio: left click creates, right click destroys when inside, middle click clears&#xD;&#xA;Dispose introduced at the chapter's end as the third reserved color (alongside Create white and Update gray) — a member operation that runs automatically when an instance is destroyed&#xD;&#xA;Brief, honest acknowledgment that Dispose also coordinates with IDisposable for resource cleanup, with a link out to the Gray Book for that deeper case&#xD;&#xA;Carrying example: The full Particle app, interactive. By the end of this chapter, the reader has a complete working application built entirely with Records.&#xD;&#xA;Back-references: ch. 11 (Process Nodes, what they can't do), ch. 27 (Mouse Input), ch. 28 (Collision), ch. 29 (Triggers &amp; Flops)">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="FMuSZGAZyXvN9GtyyXAzFH" Bounds="127,21332,589,46" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="44. Mutable Collections">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">22</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="JsuQZACFHg6OxPYMbChXms" Bounds="116,22470,438,681" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Role: The collection chapter. The reader learns that the collection question is independent of the Record/Class question.&#xD;&#xA;Core idea: Spreads are immutable. SpreadBuilder, MutableDictionary, and ObservableCollection are mutable collection types that let your application scale to hundreds or thousands of instances without the per-frame copy cost of immutable collections. Crucially, this choice is separate from whether your individual objects are Records or Classes — the two axes are independent.&#xD;&#xA;What lands:&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The performance argument for mutability, properly located: at the collection level, where it actually matters, not at the individual-object level where it usually doesn't&#xD;&#xA;SpreadBuilder as the mutable alternative to Spread, with ToImmutable to convert back when you need to hand the collection downstream&#xD;&#xA;MutableDictionary as the mutable alternative to Dictionary (lands chapter 26's promise)&#xD;&#xA;ObservableCollection and its reactive nature, briefly — enough for the reader to recognize when they'd reach for it&#xD;&#xA;The independence of the two mutability questions: the reader can have an immutable Spread of mutable Classes, or a mutable SpreadBuilder of immutable Records, and both are sensible for different reasons&#xD;&#xA;The &quot;explicitly clear every frame&quot; pattern for SpreadBuilder — keeping the same instance rather than recreating it each frame, which is where the performance benefit actually comes from&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Examples: Small, dry demonstrations only — a Spread vs SpreadBuilder side-by-side showing how each handles repeated additions, a brief look at MutableDictionary, a glimpse of ObservableCollection. No carrying example, no application context. Pure mechanics.&#xD;&#xA;Back-references: ch. 19 (Spreads — the immutable foundation), ch. 26 (Dictionaries — explicitly lands its forward promise about collections and OOP)&#xD;&#xA;Closing pointer: Links to TobyK's Spread vs SpreadBuilder tutorial (number 6 in his series) for the detailed performance demonstration.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="GhbHSev1OGRPrdqs0Jqa4X" Bounds="-566,8709,401,434" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Role: Mechanics chapter. How Records gain behavior.&#xD;&#xA;Core idea: A Record's operations are its verbs. Every step in the node graph produces a new Record with new data — visible through tooltips, just like inspecting a value along a chain of arithmetic nodes.&#xD;&#xA;What lands:&#xD;&#xA;Default operations on a Record: Create, Split&#xD;&#xA;Custom operations: setters, getters, anything you define&#xD;&#xA;Walkthrough of defining a custom operation&#xD;&#xA;Each operation copies the Record forward — the reader can hover anywhere in the chain and see the current state&#xD;&#xA;Static vs member operations: the visual difference and when each makes sense, taught through what the reader sees rather than textual-coding analogies&#xD;&#xA;The Particle gets defined here as a Record with custom operations&#xD;&#xA;Carrying example: The Particle defined as a Record. By the end of this chapter, the Particle has properties, custom operations, and can be inspected end-to-end through tooltips.&#xD;&#xA;Back-references: ch. 24 (Update &amp; Create)&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="AXqKqemwzcZOhVYIrcmETO" Bounds="629,17957,404,126" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="- creating a class&#xD;&#xA;- switching between record/class&#xD;&#xA;- explaining orange socks for mutable and strategies to resolve&#xD;&#xA;- Dashed link&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="PaGS9RD00XvP3byg06ezBW" Bounds="582,22579,374,96" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="- Counterpart of a Spread: SpreadBuilder&#xD;&#xA;- Counterpart of a Dictionary: MutableDictionary&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="SS1aNRXYRyaO75FaZqTODK" Bounds="1028,2770,397,207" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="COMMENTS&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You pick a Process when you:&#xD;&#xA;- want to create modules of your patches for a better overview&#xD;&#xA;- want to create abstract functionality into re-usable components&#xD;&#xA;- want to split your application into several documents, for example when working as a team&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A process is an object and can be created and used several times&#xD;&#xA;Has to be placed manually in the patch&#xD;&#xA;What if we could create objects dynamically during the runtime?">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
