          <Pad Id="OuMwAq8n4M4LkdWChQvZD4" Comment="Count" Bounds="1922,20703,35,15" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" />
          <Pad Id="AJiU5qdLNnuLLU2aEJ9mxQ" Comment="Pointer" Bounds="1972,20682,35,15" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" />
          <Pad Id="RNUSY2yxAWhMUmBwuIIpNL" Bounds="127,18887,589,46" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="43. Records vs. Classes">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">22</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="N2ObRmtJeFSQRvFEC2Lwh9" Bounds="87,20321,420,633" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Role: The decision chapter. Where the trade-offs get the space they deserve.&#xD;&#xA;Core idea: Record is the default for almost everything. Class is a specialist tool with specific reasons to reach for it and real costs to be aware of. This chapter teaches the reader to choose deliberately, not by reflex.&#xD;&#xA;What lands:&#xD;&#xA;The decision tree, derived from chapter 42's mutability mechanic rather than presented as a memorized list — Process, Record, Class as three flavors with distinct purposes&#xD;&#xA;Records-as-default framing: the functional bias of VL, why Record is the right starting point&#xD;&#xA;The two genuine Class use cases for a beginner: calling operations from multiple locations without storing back to a pad, and performance optimization&#xD;&#xA;An honest acknowledgment of what switching to Class costs: Changed nodes and Cache regions only detect reference changes (not mutations), Undo and SampleHold need workarounds, order of operations with parallel modifications can become unclear&#xD;&#xA;A specific small demonstration of Changed not firing on Class mutation&#xD;&#xA;Common mistakes: reaching for Class when you don't need it, conflating object mutability with collection mutability&#xD;&#xA;Note that collections are a separate question, previewing chapter 44&#xD;&#xA;Examples: Small, dry demonstrations only — a Changed-vs-mutation patch to make the cost visible, possibly one more for order-of-operations. No carrying example, no application context.&#xD;&#xA;Back-references: ch. 42 (Classes and Mutability — the mechanic this chapter builds on)&#xD;&#xA;Closing pointer: Links to TobyK's seven-part Mutability tutorial series for readers who want the deep dive into change detection, snapshots, order of operations, and the architectural implications.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="NRHl5uR5he8OeEMFc2Pto0" Bounds="127,24918,442,822" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Role: The closing chapter. The synthesis of Part V — where Process, Record, and Class are revealed as one family.&#xD;&#xA;Core idea: Records and Classes can be enabled to behave as Process nodes — placed directly in the patch, with Create running on placement, Update running each frame, and Dispose running on removal. This reveals that Process, Record, and Class are not three separate things but a small family of choices about how to use the same underlying machinery. The chapter also gives Dispose, briefly introduced in chapter 41, its proper home.&#xD;&#xA;What lands:&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The &quot;Enable as Process&quot; toggle in the Patch Explorer, for both Records and Classes&#xD;&#xA;What changes when enabled: the type can be placed in the patch as a node; Create runs on placement, Update runs each frame, Dispose runs on removal&#xD;&#xA;When to reach for this mode: singletons, place-and-forget objects, fixed-set instances&#xD;&#xA;When not to: when you need many instances created and destroyed at runtime — the patterns from chapters 40 and 41&#xD;&#xA;The family revealed: Process, Record, and Class as one continuum; a Process node is essentially a type with Process behavior enabled by default and no runtime instances&#xD;&#xA;Dispose properly demonstrated: now that we have a Process-enabled type, Dispose has a clear firing moment — when the node is removed from the patch. Shown on both a Record and a Class, with a brief note that the underlying reason for the dispose moment differs subtly between the two (Class loses its last reference; Record's placement is removed)&#xD;&#xA;Closing reflection: a brief look back across Part V — what the reader can do now that they couldn't at chapter 37&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Examples: Small, dry demonstrations only — a Record with Process enabled, placed in the patch, with a counter or LFO operation that runs automatically. A second small example showing Dispose firing. No carrying example.&#xD;&#xA;Back-references: ch. 11 (Process Nodes — completes the circle), ch. 41 (Dispose introduced there, properly landed here)&#xD;&#xA;Closing pointer: Links to TobyK's tutorial 7 (Architecture and Design Patterns) and the design pattern resources he points at, for readers ready to think about application architecture beyond what this tutorial covers.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Node Bounds="1666,9694,84,19" Id="LA8RGHiC7EsNd61qsv0sCZ">
            <p:NodeReference LastCategoryFullName="Main" LastDependency="Explanation 40. Member and Static Operations - TODO.vl">
              <Choice Kind="NodeFlag" Name="Node" Fixed="true" />
              <Choice Kind="ProcessAppFlag" Name="ColoredCircle" />
            </p:NodeReference>
            <Pin Id="QdNxtMpaYUPP4Bv56Y3ytj" Name="Node Context" Kind="InputPin" IsHidden="true" />
            <Pin Id="GVXHMan8H3JNpezz1Bu9ZC" Name="Color" Kind="InputPin" />
            <Pin Id="G7dlnL2LUY6PwQ5j4vb5HR" Name="Stroke Width" Kind="InputPin" />
            <Pin Id="Hfrzt1A8Ho5Lo3E6QaefJD" Name="Grow" Kind="InputPin" />
            <Pin Id="SrImeDl9lEzOWfofAINO78" Name="Shrink" Kind="InputPin" />
            <Pin Id="P5G2174uC7IPHhuAjYI6DT" Name="Output" Kind="OutputPin" />
          </Node>
          <Pad Id="TXDuflNwuAdMwpy29JcYyX" Bounds="1595,9118,401,434" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="After the cliffhanger of the last chapter I want to at least introduce you to the concept of Member Operations which are already an important base to understand in preparation for object-oriented programming.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You already got to know two operations which are enabled by default in every patch and process node: Update and Create. You can use them anytime to specify if a part of your patch should be executed every frame during runtime or just one frame when the patch or process is initialized. &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Operations like Create and Update which belong to an object in VL, in this case to a Process, are called Member Operation. As a user and designer of your own application you've been given the freedom to define as many Member Operations as you like and assign parts of your Process nodes to them. Once an operation is defined inside of the node, it is available on the outside as a trigger and can be used from there. &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In this example, we have implemented a Grow and Shrink operation - look into the definition of the ColoredCircle for further details - note that this time the definition of the Process lies on the definitions side of the patch, right where it belongs.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="PJdRbTOU7qXPHOPazOA5BK" Comment="Grow" Bounds="1721,9627,35,35" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="False">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="ImmutableTypeFlag" Name="Boolean" />
              <CategoryReference Kind="Category" Name="Primitive" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
