            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Overlay Id="MMMcTzGACzqPpA2VVWjbs3" Name="" Bounds="127,18691,4576,80">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">4</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
          <Overlay Id="GhQR5eD9nrBNwFaq4wCMVZ" Name="" Bounds="138,21160,4576,80">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">4</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
          <Overlay Id="HHtaewyKa4FMOpgq3ScAhk" Name="" Bounds="141,23394,4576,80">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">4</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
          <Pad Id="NhBvi0EhYZnPJ2UKJIw2aL" Bounds="-509,16678,515,275" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The mechanic chapter for mutability. Class enters as the mutable counterpart to Record. The reader meets the State Output pin behavior, the solid-vs-dashed link distinction, and the &quot;changes in place vs produces a copy&quot; observation through small dry demonstrations — most importantly the MyRecord/MyClass side-by-side patch where the same operations produce three colors on the Record side and one color on the Class side.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This chapter does not yet do the decision tree or the trade-off discussion. Those belong to chapter 43. Chapter 42 introduces the mechanic and the visual language; chapter 43 helps the reader choose.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="GTGbw6HeNqkMmUlx3aHbDa" Bounds="-509,16979,540,908" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**1. MyRecord and MyClass as the working example, not the Particle.** The chapter uses generic placeholder types because the demonstration is purely mechanical — the reader needs to see *just* the mutability behavior, without any application context distracting from it. Chapter 43 will rebuild the Particle as a Class for comparison; chapter 42 keeps things abstract.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**2. The State Input/Output visual was primed in chapter 39.** Chapter 39 already mentioned that the State Input and State Output are &quot;not visually connected&quot; inside a Record operation, planting the visual cue before naming it. Chapter 42 picks it up — the Class version *is* connected — and the reader recognizes the pattern they have been seeing for chapters. This is deliberate scaffolding.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**3. Solid vs dashed links also get explicit naming.** Up to this point, the reader has only ever seen solid links. The dashed link is genuinely new visual vocabulary. The chapter names it, explains what it means, and lets the reader see it on the demonstration patch.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**4. The chapter does not present Records and Classes as equal alternatives.** The closing column (&quot;What This Means&quot;) explicitly previews that &quot;Class is not free&quot; — the reader leaves chapter 42 knowing that the trade-off discussion is the heart of chapter 43, and that they should not switch to Class casually.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**5. No decision tree in this chapter.** That's chapter 43's job. Chapter 42 only teaches the mechanic and the visual language.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**6. No Particle rebuild here either.** That's also chapter 43, where the reader can see the comparison in their own working code.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**7. Length.** Roughly six columns of body text plus two live patches (one inspection element, one full side-by-side demonstration). Comparable in scope to chapter 24 (Update &amp; Create) — a focused mechanic chapter with one central demonstration.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="JgVOUoTaNLFOWiUG6cxzuB" Bounds="-476,18906,506,198" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The decision chapter. Where the trade-offs get the space they deserve. The reader learns to choose between Record and Class deliberately, with an understanding of what's given up to gain mutability. The Particle from chapter 41 gets rebuilt as a Class for explicit comparison — the reader feels the wiring relief and feels what's been traded for it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is also where the decision tree lives, but as a *derivation* from the mutability mechanic rather than as a memorized list of rules.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="MWBZKJBQB5EO8hpPD3SfYG" Bounds="-476,19118,540,908" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**1. The decision tree is not a list of rules.** Each section of this chapter is an argument the reader follows — Records are the default because of how VL works; switch to Class for two specific reasons; here is what you give up; here is what it looks like in practice. By the end the reader has derived the decision rather than memorized it. The tree exists implicitly but is not presented as a numbered checklist.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**2. The &quot;Records as Default&quot; column does heavy lifting.** This is the conceptual foundation of the chapter. Records are not &quot;one of two equal options&quot; — they are the natural fit for VL's functional design. Naming this clearly sets up everything that follows: switching to Class is *deviating from the default*, and the chapter justifies when that deviation makes sense.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**3. The Particle rebuild is the chapter's centerpiece.** This is where the abstract trade-offs become concrete. The reader sees their own working code from chapter 41 reshaped as a Class and can feel both the wiring relief and the loss of change detection. The pedagogical move is that the reader is not deciding between two abstractions — they are deciding what to do with an application they actually built.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**4. The &quot;what you give up&quot; column is detailed.** This is the chapter's most important content. The reader needs to know specifically what stops working — Changed, Cache, Snapshots, SampleHold, order of operations. Naming them by name and tying them to the mutability mechanic from chapter 42 makes the cost real instead of vague.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**5. The &quot;common mistakes&quot; column has only two items.** Deliberately tight. The two mistakes named — switching for wiring relief, switching for unmeasured performance — are the ones a beginner will actually encounter. A longer list would lose the reader; two memorable warnings will stick.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**6. The collections preview.** A small column that explicitly separates the object-mutability question from the collection-mutability question. This sets up chapter 44 cleanly and prevents the common conflation where &quot;I need performance&quot; becomes &quot;I should use Classes.&quot;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**7. The closing resource is TobyK's full series.** The reader who wants to go deeper has a clear path. The forum thread is also linked because it remains the canonical reference for the question, even though this chapter has updated and reframed its answer.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**8. Length.** Roughly six columns of body text plus two substantial live patches (the Changed demonstration and the Particle rebuild). Comparable in scope to chapter 42 — these two chapters are a natural pair.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**9. What this chapter does *not* do.** It does not introduce mutable collections (chapter 44). It does not introduce Process-enabled types or Dispose (chapter 45). It stays squarely on the Record-vs-Class decision and lets the reader walk away with that question settled.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="UOMrrLaPqO0Ow2kKNoiqlK" Bounds="127,16770,401,459" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="For four chapters now we have been working with Records. Every Record operation produces a new copy of the Record, with changes applied. To make changes persist, we have stored the result back into a pad — explicitly, frame by frame. This is how Records work.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;There is a second flavor of object in VL called a Class. A Class looks almost identical to a Record on the outside — properties, operations, the same Patch Explorer interface. The difference is in how operations affect the object. A Class operation does not produce a new copy. It modifies the existing object in place. The same Class instance going into an operation comes out the other side, changed.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This chapter introduces Class as a mechanic. We will see how it differs from Record in the patch, and we will look at a small demonstration that makes the difference visible. The next chapter will help you decide which one to reach for and when.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="UUGEjZovmUtP9C9YtlTsdM" Bounds="595,16784,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A Second Flavor">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="EEVMQvPFFFRLyPqDwdGCmM" Bounds="595,16826,401,330" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Record and Class are the two flavors of object you can define in VL. You define them the same way — open the Patch Explorer, type a name, choose &quot;Record&quot; or &quot;Class.&quot; You give them properties the same way. You define operations on them the same way. The Node Browser shows them with similar nodes — Create, Split, and whatever custom operations you define.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The difference is what happens when an operation modifies the object. A Record operation produces a new copy with the change applied. The original Record on the input is unchanged. A Class operation modifies the object on the input directly. There is only one instance, before and after the operation, and it is now different.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This single difference is the entire substance of the chapter. Everything else — the State Output pin, the dashed links, the explicit-store pattern disappearing — follows from it.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="IBjfLFbc3UmPwuM9YC8SHX" Bounds="1050,16781,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Defining a Class">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="GYJ8ri45RsnMTndGlrr4N7" Bounds="1050,16823,401,330" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="To define a Class, open the Patch Explorer, right-click in the document, and choose &quot;Add Class.&quot; Give it a name. Add properties. Add operations. The interface is identical to Record's.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In our example below we define a Class called MyClass and a Record called MyRecord, both with a single Color property and a single operation called SetColor that changes that property. The definitions look almost the same in the Patch Explorer — you can tell them apart by the icon next to each in the explorer's region list. The R icon marks a Record, the C icon marks a Class.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The operations look slightly different inside, though, and that is where the mechanic becomes visible.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="JOl2JXD9UFaMXndL4AexqF" Bounds="1487,16788,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The State Output Pin">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="VfiNmjokksOMwPtJpFyNU1" Bounds="1487,16830,401,330" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Open the SetColor operation on the Record and look at it. State Input pin on the left, State Output pin on the right. Inside the operation, the new Color value is assigned to the Color property of the State Output. The State Input and the State Output are not visually connected. This tells you that the Record coming out is not the same Record that went in — it is a new copy.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Now open the SetColor operation on the Class. The same two pins, State Input and State Output. But inside, the State Input and the State Output are visually connected to each other. The Class coming out is the same Class that went in. There is no copy. The assignment to Color modifies the Class directly, and the same Class is passed through.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This visual distinction inside the operation is the first place mutability becomes a thing you can see, rather than a thing you have to remember.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="MiHn1RS3WDoL5L5GbEQjRh" Bounds="1487,17175,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=1092, y=820):**&#xD;&#xA;- A small inspection patch showing the SetColor operation for MyRecord and MyClass side-by-side, opened in nested patch views or referenced via Patch Explorer.&#xD;&#xA;- Annotation IOBoxes pointing at the State Input and State Output pins on each, noting the visual difference (not-connected vs connected).">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="P3fDb0F2ny3N879eAIUWnH" Bounds="1933,16786,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Same Operations, Different Results">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="T62AH7tU7LbMltx515bm0E" Bounds="1933,16828,402,464" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The patch below shows the central demonstration of this chapter. On the left side we have a MyRecord, created and then passed through two SetColor operations — first setting it to blue, then to red. On the right side we have a MyClass, created and passed through two SetColor operations — first blue, then red. The patches are structurally identical.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;At three points along each chain — at the start, in the middle between the two SetColor operations, and at the end — we tap in a reader that reads the current Color and displays it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Look at what the readers show. On the Record side, three different colors — white at the start, blue in the middle, red at the end. Each reader sees whatever the Record was at the point it tapped in. On the Class side, three identical colors — all three readers show red, the final value. Even the reader tapped in before the second SetColor shows red, because there is only one MyClass instance and it ends up red. The &quot;before&quot; and &quot;after&quot; states are not preserved on the Class side, because there is no copy.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is the entire mechanic in one screen. A Record holds its history along the chain because every step is a new value. A Class does not — it is one thing that changes, and every reference to it sees the latest state.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="TBbfsY4QRe9MfUEhCrPtva" Bounds="1935,17308,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=1180, width ~1400):**&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The MyRecord/MyClass side-by-side patch as we discussed earlier in the conversation:&#xD;&#xA;- Left half: `Create MyRecord` → `SetColor (blue)` → `SetColor (red)` chain. Three `Color MyRecord` reader nodes tapped in at start, middle, end. They display white, blue, red.&#xD;&#xA;- Right half: `Create MyClass` → `SetColor (blue)` → `SetColor (red)` chain. Three `Color MyClass` reader nodes tapped in at start, middle, end. They display red, red, red.&#xD;&#xA;- Links on the left are solid; links on the right are dashed.&#xD;&#xA;- An annotation pointing at the middle readers (&quot;this is where they disagree&quot;) to draw the eye to the key moment.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="JNLiFKw3uumOxiP1F6fowS" Bounds="2381,16782,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Solid and Dashed Links">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="LCDM2u0GFFwP6I1r6VJPQY" Bounds="2381,16824,402,358" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="You may have already noticed in the patch above. The links carrying MyRecord are drawn as solid lines. The links carrying MyClass are drawn as dashed lines. This is the visual signature of mutability across the entire vvvv environment.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Solid link: the value being carried is immutable. Whatever flows through this link is a value, like a number or a Record. Each step in the patch produces a new one. The link is a stream of values.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Dashed link: the value being carried is mutable. The link is not a stream of values — it is a reference to one thing. The same thing might be modified anywhere along the link's path, and every part of the patch holding this reference sees the change.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You will see this distinction throughout your patches once you start using Classes. Any link carrying a Class will be dashed. Any link carrying a Record will be solid. The patch tells you, just by looking, which kind of object is flowing through.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Mkmcvyqu4wSNKGvLkyHvRy" Bounds="2841,16783,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="What This Means">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="IZ13gsVgQwSO7fYBhYsxya" Bounds="2841,16825,404,412" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The mechanic has practical consequences that ripple through the rest of your patch. The most visible one — the explicit-store pattern from chapters 23 onward — does not apply to Classes. When you modify a Class via an operation, the change persists automatically, because the Class is the same object before and after. You do not need a pad to remember it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;That is the headline feature of Classes, and it is what makes them tempting. But it is not the whole story. There are things Records do automatically that Classes do not — change detection, snapshots, the natural clarity of order when multiple operations modify a value. These are not abstract concerns; they show up the moment you start building things with Classes that you used to build with Records.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The next chapter looks at this honestly. When to reach for a Class, when to stay with a Record, and what you give up by switching. Class is not free, and the decision is worth making deliberately.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Overlay Id="OIBKbtaL5QVLPvQRjJr5Ft" Bounds="3432,16815,383,298">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">10</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
          <Pad Id="Ea4umkTu8nhOxJ2vmofFDk" Bounds="3433,16837,216,25" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Other Resources">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">12</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="AnDNLFwjK0EONNRUX85T07" Bounds="3433,16868,197,19" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Gray Book on Types">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="CN43nKpkWe5PQAutYu20aB" Bounds="3433,16887,360,54" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://thegraybook.vvvv.org/reference/language/types.html">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Link</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="NWqaDnkb76NOxRbR7SDHZi" Bounds="3433,16943,358,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Mutability in vvvv Gamma (Record vs Class) by TobyK">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="M2VZRSpEJNMMQv89vYAJu1" Bounds="3433,16972,360,54" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://youtu.be/zGufG64WSF4">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Link</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="H8vc6uyGOecNfacSQsxXu3" Bounds="127,18977,401,459" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The last chapter showed the mechanic. This chapter helps you choose. When should you reach for a Class instead of a Record? The honest answer is — less often than you might think. Records are the default for almost everything you will build in VL, and the cases where Classes are the right move are specific and worth recognizing.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;We will look at the genuine reasons to choose a Class, the real costs of that choice, and how it plays out in a concrete case — the Particle from chapter 41, rebuilt as a Class so you can compare them side by side. By the end of this chapter you will be able to make the call confidently, instead of switching based on a hunch.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="LHHUJ7nWdmFO6hwP8RQeGj" Bounds="595,18991,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Records as Default">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="KOaPcLURm09ORajl7Lfeto" Bounds="595,19033,405,467" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="VL is a functional environment at its core. Values flow through your patch from one operation to the next, each step producing a new result, and the language is designed around that flow. Records fit this design naturally. They are values. They behave the way numbers, strings, and built-in types behave — passed through operations, transformed, copied along the way.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is why Record is the default choice for almost any custom data type. The functional model is not just a style — it is how the rest of the language works. Change detection assumes values flow through the patch and that new ones differ from old ones. Caching assumes the same. Snapshots and history rely on the fact that each value is preserved at the moment it existed.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;When you use a Record, you get all of this automatically. You can detect when the Record changed. You can cache results. You can hold a snapshot and compare it later. None of this requires extra work — it is what the language does when you let values flow through it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The starting question is therefore not &quot;should I use a Class?&quot; The starting question is &quot;do I have a specific reason not to use a Record?&quot; If you cannot point at one, you almost certainly want a Record.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="B5QKptideIANa90z2MaWAi" Bounds="1049,18971,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="When to Switch to Class">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="BoZ4tBgwfVdMiFcGfRLGR7" Bounds="1049,19013,405,548" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="There are two reasons that are worth recognizing as a beginner.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The first is access from multiple locations. Suppose you have an object that needs to be modified from several different places in your patch — one part of the application changes its Position, another changes its Color, a third changes its State. With a Record, each modification has to read the current Record from its pad, apply the change, and store it back. If three places are doing this in the same frame, you have to be careful about which one runs first, and the wiring gets dense. With a Class, each location simply calls the appropriate operation directly. The Class modifies itself, and every part of the patch sees the change immediately. The wiring disappears.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The second is performance. A Record copies itself on every modification. For a single Record this cost is invisible. For a thousand Records all updating every frame, the cost can add up — a thousand new copies created and old copies discarded each frame. A Class has no such cost; it modifies itself in place. If you have measured a performance problem and traced it to object copying, switching to a Class is one tool that can help.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Notice the qualifier on the second reason — if you have measured. Switching to Classes for performance without measuring first is one of the most common mistakes. The cost of Record copying is usually invisible, and the cost of giving up Records' features for an imagined performance gain is real.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="FQZUjV2ga3JLyhXcqN0LiF" Bounds="1497,18969,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="What You Give Up">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="F2OAbPjTTrYMEbez5BuuWP" Bounds="1497,19011,404,619" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The relief of not wiring back to a pad comes at a price. The features that Records give you automatically do not work the same way with Classes.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The most visible loss is change detection. The Changed node and Cache regions watch a link for changes. With a Record link, every modification produces a new value, and Changed fires. With a Class link, the link carries the same reference even when the underlying object is modified, and Changed does not fire. The Gray Book is honest about this — Changed could really be called ChangedReference. It detects when a new Class instance arrives, not when the existing one is modified.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Snapshots and Undo work similarly. With Records, holding a snapshot of a value means holding that value — the Record will not change because it cannot. With Classes, holding a &quot;snapshot&quot; means holding a reference, and the referenced Class can be modified between when you took the snapshot and when you tried to use it. SampleHold has the same issue.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Order of operations becomes less obvious too. With Records, modifications happen serially because you explicitly wire them. With Classes, two parts of the patch can both modify the same Class instance in the same frame, and the order is sometimes determined by the F9 execution order rather than anything visible in the patch. The wiring relief that drew you to Classes is also the loss of clarity about when each modification happens.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You can work around all of these — there are patterns using BehaviourSubject, ticket counters, and explicit Clone operations. But the workarounds are advanced material, and they exist to recover what Records give you for free.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="HguDsSjUZliNnVDTojYRc5" Bounds="1500,19648,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=1092, y=920):**&#xD;&#xA;A small dry demonstration of Changed not firing:&#xD;&#xA;- A Record in a pad, with SetColor called on it via a button click. A Changed node downstream fires each time the button is clicked.&#xD;&#xA;- A Class in a pad, with SetColor called on it via a button click. A Changed node downstream does not fire — the reference stays the same even though the Color is now different.&#xD;&#xA;- IOBoxes labeled to make the contrast unmissable.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="HOzbIEirGejP3iArKFq9BU" Bounds="233,19530,355,127" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="List and specify more intermediate reasons why to switch to a class! But only demonstrate the pad-less thingie.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Rph7j2AMULxLlSIllBe0Ud" Bounds="1967,18972,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Particle, Rebuilt">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="HrOMSKEbzX9Pqpu2fMdVCq" Bounds="1967,19014,404,557" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Now let's look at this on the application from chapter 41. The Particle was a Record. We stored a Spread&lt;Particle&gt; in a pad. Each frame we read the spread, called Update on each Particle, and stored the spread back into the pad. The explicit-store pattern was everywhere.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The patch below shows the same application rebuilt with Particle as a Class. The structure is almost the same — a Spread&lt;Particle&gt;, a ForEach, calls to Update on each Particle. But the storing-back pattern is gone. Update modifies each Class in place. The spread still has to be in a pad so it persists across frames, but the individual Particles do not need to be stored back into the spread — they are the same Particle each frame, just with new Position.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Compare the two patches. The Class version is shorter. The data flow is less complicated. The wiring relief is real.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;But look at what is no longer there. With the Record version, you could put a Changed node on the spread and detect when the application state changed in any way. With the Class version, that no longer works — the spread is the same spread even when its Particles' positions are all different. If you wanted change-driven logic in your application, the Record version supported it. The Class version requires you to add explicit notification.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Most beginner applications never use this feature, and the wiring relief feels like a clear win. Most intermediate applications do use it, sometimes without realizing. The trade is worth making with eyes open.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="FmwRVSqli03OcS8qkeifgX" Bounds="1970,19604,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=1200, width ~1400):**&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Side-by-side patches:&#xD;&#xA;- **Left half:** Particle-as-Record from chapter 41 (or simplified version of it) — pad with Spread&lt;Particle&gt;, ForEach with Update, store back to pad.&#xD;&#xA;- **Right half:** Same application, with Particle now a Class. ForEach with Update but no store-back at the Particle level. The spread pad is still there but the inner wiring is reduced.&#xD;&#xA;- Both produce identical visual output on their respective SkiaRenderers.&#xD;&#xA;- Annotations pointing at the wiring difference, and another set pointing at where Changed-style detection would or would not work.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="SmhQAuk4u04OTIWlIMcQRa" Bounds="2452,18968,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Common Mistakes">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="J1K02K43VxINfLlhdnxSHI" Bounds="2452,19010,404,321" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Two patterns to watch for, both common when patchers first discover Classes.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Reaching for a Class because the Record wiring feels verbose. The wiring is not noise — it is the patch making the data flow explicit. Verbosity that you can read is more valuable than terseness that hides behavior. Switch to a Class when you have a specific reason from the previous columns, not because the Record version &quot;feels&quot; heavier than it should.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Reaching for a Class for performance without measuring. The cost of Record copying is usually negligible. If you are unsure whether you have a performance problem, you do not have one. If you do have one, measure first — find out where the cost actually is. The answer is often a different patch structure or a mutable collection (chapter 44), not a switch from Record to Class.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="IDgbchtSoMjPYw6sIeiDwr" Bounds="2906,18965,412,34" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Collections Are a Separate Question">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="VuAMDMmPMMPPeT1P9xpmTK" Bounds="2906,19007,404,321" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="One last thing worth naming before the next chapter. The choice between Record and Class is about individual objects. There is a separate choice about collections — whether to use Spread (immutable) or SpreadBuilder (mutable) for the container that holds many of them. These two questions are independent.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You can have an immutable Spread of mutable Classes. You can have a mutable SpreadBuilder of immutable Records. Both are sensible patterns for different reasons. Chapter 44 looks at the collection question on its own terms — when to use mutable collection types, what they cost, and how they relate to object mutability.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;For now, just notice that &quot;I need performance&quot; rarely means &quot;I should switch from Records to Classes.&quot; It often means &quot;I should use a mutable collection.&quot; The distinction matters.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Overlay Id="VvL6B7FvPNqMNxt433Sqcl" Bounds="3522,18953,383,298">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">10</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
          <Pad Id="N8YA1xBFyOYQdgv36KfOWF" Bounds="3523,18975,216,25" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Other Resources">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">12</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="GmSXdlkD7ljLPGp2mJ4VlW" Bounds="3523,19006,355,19" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Mutability in vvvv Gamma — full tutorial series by TobyK">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="CxLpmodMETpOU8GKvcNt2g" Bounds="3523,19025,360,54" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://youtu.be/zGufG64WSF4">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Link</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="OoKT0y2Tnr3NHhlEK1kYiE" Bounds="3523,19081,358,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="When to pick a Process, a Record or a Class">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="U61Ewiqlbr0LpqCF2k8dBu" Bounds="3523,19110,360,54" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://forum.vvvv.org/t/when-to-pick-a-process-a-record-or-a-class/21624">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Link</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="EqvXjlSlqYxNL8JNAKl2WW" Bounds="-496,21380,506,198" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The collection chapter. The reader learns that the collection mutability question is independent of the Record/Class question, and meets the mutable alternatives to Spread and Dictionary. The performance argument for mutability lives here, properly located at the collection level where it actually matters, rather than at the individual-object level where it usually doesn't.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is also where chapter 26's promise about collections and OOP gets landed explicitly.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="II4W6Rg7utdM70U2sLaCAH" Bounds="-496,21592,540,908" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**1. The independence framing comes first.** The opening paragraph and Column 1 both hammer the point that collection mutability is a separate question from object mutability. This is the most important thing the reader leaves the chapter with — without it, &quot;I need performance&quot; becomes &quot;I should use Classes,&quot; which the previous chapter just told them not to do.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**2. The four combinations as a structural lens.** Rather than listing each mutable collection type as if they were independent tools, the chapter positions them as the answer to one axis of a two-axis decision. This grounds the chapter in the framework the reader has been building.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**3. SpreadBuilder gets the most space.** It is the most commonly reached-for mutable collection and the one that maps most directly onto Spread. MutableDictionary follows the same pattern as a brief parallel. ObservableCollection gets one column because it is genuinely more advanced and most readers will not need it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**4. The &quot;clear each frame&quot; pattern.** Highlighted in its own column at the bottom because it is the single most common mistake when first using SpreadBuilder. Without naming it explicitly, readers will rebuild a fresh SpreadBuilder each frame and wonder why their performance did not improve.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**5. Chapter 26's promise gets landed.** The reader sees that the &quot;collections become more interesting with custom types&quot; pointer from chapter 26 is fulfilled here — both via MutableDictionary's relationship to Dictionary and via the four-combinations framing.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**6. No new mechanics or syntax beyond the basic operations.** The chapter assumes the reader knows Spread, Dictionary, pads, and basic operations. Everything else flows from analogy with what they already have.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**7. Length.** Roughly seven columns of body text plus two live patches. Comparable in scope to chapter 43 — these two chapters are the deeper-cut pair, more substantial than 40 and 41 because the conceptual content is denser.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="MQqk2a0rRs6OsGwTuuh6hd" Bounds="-442,23610,506,198" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The closing chapter. The synthesis. The reveal that Process, Record, and Class are not three separate things but one family. The reader meets the &quot;Enable as Process&quot; toggle, sees the small example of a Record running automatically when placed in the patch, and watches Dispose finally fire visibly. The chapter closes with a brief look back across Part V and a forward pointer to what comes after.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="GM2MSUNgbEQNOZICUVAbn7" Bounds="-442,23822,540,908" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**1. The chapter does three things in sequence.** First it teaches the &quot;Enable as Process&quot; toggle and shows it on a small Counter. Second it uses the same mode to demonstrate Dispose finally firing visibly. Third it reveals the Process-Record-Class family and looks back at Part V. The three jobs reinforce each other — the Counter shows how the mode works, the Logger shows what Dispose finally does, and the family reveal makes both make sense in the context of everything that came before.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**2. The Counter and Logger are deliberately small.** Two of the dryest, most minimal examples in all of Part V. The chapter's job is synthesis, not demonstration of complex behavior. Small examples keep the focus on the conceptual unification.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**3. The &quot;two reasons for Dispose to fire&quot; column is honest but light.** This is the most technically nuanced moment in the chapter — Records and Classes have Dispose for slightly different underlying reasons. The column names this but does not dive deep. The reader who wants to understand resource cleanup more thoroughly has TobyK's tutorial and the Gray Book linked.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**4. The &quot;When to Reach For It&quot; column is direct.** One of these in your patch: enable Process. Many of these in a spread: don't. This is the practical rule the reader needs, named clearly.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**5. &quot;Looking Back at Part V&quot; does narrative work.** It is the only part of Part V that explicitly summarizes what came before. The chapter has earned the right to do this — it is the closer, and a brief look back is what a closer should provide.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**6. The forward pointer is gentle.** &quot;The next part of the tutorial — once you continue — picks up from here and looks at how your applications begin to interact with everything outside themselves.&quot; This previews the Emergent Mind section without naming it explicitly. The reader knows there is more coming if they want it; they are not promised a specific timeline.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**7. The cosmological theme is honored implicitly.** Part V is &quot;The Origin of Life.&quot; This chapter closes by saying the reader's program has become a thing that can run, persist, and end on its own — Create, Update, Dispose as a complete life arc. The metaphor lands without being labored.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**8. Length.** Roughly nine columns of body text plus two live patches (Counter and Logger). This is the longest chapter in Part V apart from chapter 41, which is appropriate — a closer should be substantial. Comparable in scope to chapter 11 (Process Nodes) and chapter 41 (Use Case II), the other two structural anchor points.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**9. What this chapter does *not* do.** It does not introduce new advanced topics like resource management, IDisposable in detail, or design patterns beyond a passing mention. It does not introduce new mutability concepts beyond what's been established. Its job is synthesis and closure, and it stays focused on that.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="NjaFDoWed6XOcIS0ejBpVo" Bounds="127,21409,401,459" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The last two chapters were about whether your objects should be Records or Classes. This chapter is about something different but related — whether the collections holding them should be immutable or mutable. The two questions sound similar, but they are independent, and confusing them is the single most common mistake in this corner of VL.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Every collection type you have used so far in this tutorial — Spread, Dictionary — has been immutable. You add an element by producing a new collection with the element included. You remove an element by producing a new collection without it. The original collection is unchanged, and you store the new one back. This is the same pattern you have used with Records for the last few chapters.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;For most applications this works fine. But when you have many elements changing rapidly — hundreds or thousands of particles each frame, a constantly-updating set of objects in a game — the cost of producing a new collection every step starts to show. For those cases there are mutable collection types. They change in place, like Classes do.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="TUzTi2xBIQFOKBauYzRUTj" Bounds="595,21423,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A Separate Axis">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Ss563TiW7vpLWctSxsM447" Bounds="595,21465,405,467" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The mutability of the collection and the mutability of its contents are two different questions. You can mix them freely. The four combinations are all valid and each makes sense for different reasons.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;An immutable Spread of immutable Records — what you have been using throughout this tutorial. Values flowing through, every modification producing a new collection.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;An immutable Spread of mutable Classes — useful when you want to control when the collection itself changes, but the individual objects need to be modified from multiple places.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A mutable SpreadBuilder of immutable Records — useful when you want fast modification of the collection (adding, removing, clearing) but the individual elements stay as values.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A mutable SpreadBuilder of mutable Classes — the most mutable option, useful when both axes need to change rapidly.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The choice is not Records-or-Classes; the choice is two independent questions. This chapter looks at the collection side.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Kkbg8LJZJwcLapdTFQBCIV" Bounds="1040,21424,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="SpreadBuilder">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="CPcIWFxt1xRP747TwNlzqS" Bounds="1040,21466,405,301" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="SpreadBuilder is the mutable counterpart to Spread. Where a Spread is a value — adding to it produces a new Spread — a SpreadBuilder is a mutable container that you modify in place. You add elements with Add, remove them with RemoveSliceAt, clear it all with Clear, and the SpreadBuilder itself stays the same instance throughout.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;When you need to hand the contents to something that expects a regular Spread — a ForEach, a downstream operation, a renderer — you call ToImmutable on the SpreadBuilder. This produces a snapshot Spread of its current state. The SpreadBuilder remains, and you can keep modifying it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The patch on the right shows a small example. A SpreadBuilder in a pad, with Add fired by a Bang. Each click adds a number to the collection. ToImmutable produces a current Spread for inspection.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="ESlMAAOkTRcMQL1gctKwuD" Bounds="1040,21806,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=592, y=720):**&#xD;&#xA;- A SpreadBuilder&lt;Float&gt; in a pad.&#xD;&#xA;- A Bang button feeding Add with a random Float value.&#xD;&#xA;- A ToImmutable downstream producing a Spread&lt;Float&gt;.&#xD;&#xA;- An IOBox showing the current Spread.&#xD;&#xA;- A second Bang for Clear, which empties the SpreadBuilder.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="D8FITJJYNwJMvz2bMY8fTW" Bounds="1497,21417,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="MutableDictionary">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="NMBvAY33PkjLDqKNfUw5NY" Bounds="1497,21459,405,350" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="MutableDictionary is the mutable counterpart to Dictionary, which you met in chapter 26. The relationship is the same as SpreadBuilder's relationship to Spread. A Dictionary is a value — every modification produces a new one. A MutableDictionary changes in place.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You use the same operations you know — Add or SetValue with a key, Remove, Clear — but the MutableDictionary itself remains the same instance throughout. When you need a regular Dictionary for downstream use, ToImmutable gives you a snapshot.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is what chapter 26 was pointing at when it said collections become more interesting once you have your own data types. A MutableDictionary&lt;String, Particle&gt; — a lookup table keyed by name, with Particles as values, all modifiable in place — is exactly the kind of structure many applications need. You can grow it, shrink it, and modify its values without ever producing a copy of the dictionary itself.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="TaGNykPgQjcLSQpi95zh4l" Bounds="1497,21849,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=1092, y=750):**&#xD;&#xA;- A MutableDictionary&lt;String, Float&gt; in a pad.&#xD;&#xA;- IOBoxes for a String key and a Float value, feeding SetValue.&#xD;&#xA;- A Bang to fire SetValue.&#xD;&#xA;- A second Bang for Remove, with a key input.&#xD;&#xA;- ToImmutable downstream, an IOBox showing the current Dictionary contents.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="MBEcwXQcKo3LTJD3Epjpdn" Bounds="1948,21433,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="ObservableCollection">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="BsarjK5lzIgPUtyaZnN53W" Bounds="1948,21475,405,350" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="There is a third mutable collection type worth knowing about — ObservableCollection. It is a mutable spread-like collection, but with one extra feature. It emits a notification every time it changes — when an element is added, removed, or modified. This is useful when other parts of your patch need to react to changes in the collection without polling for them.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This connects to one of the things we discussed in chapter 43 about what you give up by using Classes — change detection becomes manual. ObservableCollection is one of the tools you can use to recover that capability. It is also the foundation of reactive patterns more generally, where parts of an application respond to events instead of checking values each frame.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;For most applications you will not need this. For applications that already use reactive patterns, ObservableCollection is the right starting point.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Fn1mYLf5gtcODqkRmp3jSC" Bounds="2396,21421,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Four Combinations">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="JTYC10ko8TFLAY5oNjK7u0" Bounds="2396,21463,412,499" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Going back to where this chapter started — four combinations, each with its own niche.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Immutable Spread of Records is the default. Use it whenever you can, which is most of the time. Everything in VL is designed to flow this way, and you keep all the features Records give you automatically.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Immutable Spread of Classes is useful when the collection itself is stable but individual objects need to be modified from elsewhere. You still get change detection on the spread when the spread changes, but the Classes inside can be updated in place. A common pattern for objects with internal state that updates each frame.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Mutable SpreadBuilder of Records is useful when you are adding or removing elements rapidly but each element is still a value. Particle systems where particles get added and removed but each particle is itself a stable record of position and properties. This is often the right pick when you have measured a performance problem with Spread-of-Records.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Mutable SpreadBuilder of Classes is the most mutable option and the most performant for many-element, fast-changing collections. It is also the option that gives up the most of Records' features — neither the collection nor the elements support change detection in the standard way. Reach for it when you need it, not before.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Sci2KkjhZC7Py8eZBx0WcB" Bounds="2873,21426,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="When to Reach For Them">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="VrhOtFDyPTzLscTcNy29yA" Bounds="2873,21468,405,447" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The honest signal for switching to a mutable collection is the same as for switching to a Class — measure first. If your application runs at the framerate you need with an immutable Spread, you do not need a SpreadBuilder. If it does not, profile to find out where the cost actually is before assuming the answer is the collection type.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;That said, there are situations where mutable collections are almost always the right call from the start.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Collections that change every frame and have hundreds or thousands of elements — particle systems, agents in a simulation, points in a constantly-updating dataset. The cost of producing a new immutable collection every frame is real at that scale.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Collections that are accumulated incrementally — appending log entries, collecting measurements, building up a result over many frames. The accumulation pattern is what SpreadBuilder is designed for.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Collections that need to support reactive patterns explicitly — UI lists where elements appear and disappear, where downstream components watch for changes. ObservableCollection fits naturally here.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="DU7J4L8WasHQKSYTDfDFTO" Bounds="3357,21429,402,32" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A Pattern to Know: Clear Each Frame">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="TGWaNbdPDEQPIS6W8RKFmz" Bounds="3357,21471,405,447" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="One pattern with SpreadBuilder is worth naming because it confuses people the first time they hit it. If you want to rebuild a SpreadBuilder's contents every frame — for example, recomputing a list from scratch each tick — the temptation is to create a new SpreadBuilder each frame.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This defeats the entire purpose. Creating a new SpreadBuilder each frame is exactly the per-frame allocation cost you switched to a SpreadBuilder to avoid. Instead, keep the same SpreadBuilder instance in a pad. At the start of each frame, call Clear on it to empty it. Then add the new contents. The SpreadBuilder is the same instance throughout — only its contents change.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is the pattern that gives mutable collections their actual performance benefit. The collection persists; only the data inside changes.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Overlay Id="Ii8WaakedqYK9XoybNlGji" Bounds="3868,21416,383,298">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">10</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
          <Pad Id="IzpWEo9AblUOTYS0oCcKdN" Bounds="3869,21438,216,25" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Other Resources">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">12</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="DDMYpkgPhnQM5ktIrrXhhi" Bounds="3869,21469,355,42" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Spread vs SpreadBuilder and Performance of Immutable Collections — TobyK">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="CV0dhAK0rHpLkK5Zw3lcOA" Bounds="3869,21508,357,19" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://youtu.be/zGufG64WSF4">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Link</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="By3BSeleZBDOfgXt1TzIOt" Bounds="3869,21544,358,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Gray Book on Collections">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="A1LEzby6q9tMktrNTkdtAy" Bounds="3869,21573,360,54" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://thegraybook.vvvv.org/reference/language/types.html">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Link</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="TctqS7T8wAYProQlFuhEz4" Bounds="127,23701,401,459" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="We have come a long way in Part V. We started with the recognition that Process nodes from chapter 11 were already objects — we just had not called them that. We then learned to define our own — Records first, then Classes — and built complete applications with both. We also learned when to choose each, and how to think about collections separately.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In this final chapter we close the circle. Records and Classes can themselves become Process nodes, with a single setting in the Patch Explorer. When you flip it, your type can be placed directly in the patch, its Create runs on placement, its Update runs each frame, and its Dispose runs when removed. This was always available — we just did not use it, because the rest of Part V was about types as data, managed by hand.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is also where Dispose finally makes full sense. We mentioned it in chapter 41 as the third reserved-color operation. Here we will see it fire visibly, on both a Record and a Class — for slightly different reasons, which is the last small detail of the family.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="SgWs5Wcc7pHOlP1pivvWnA" Bounds="595,23715,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A Mode We Have Not Used">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="MkSg3JumTwPNWxB4qVBKi7" Bounds="595,23757,405,467" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Throughout Part V we have used Records and Classes as data. We created instances by calling Create, modified them by calling other operations, drew them by calling Draw, and stored them in pads or spreads to keep them around between frames. Every operation we used, we called by hand — placing nodes in the patch, wiring inputs, deciding when each one would run.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;There is another way to use a Record or Class. You can place the type itself directly into your patch, like a Process node. When you do, the type behaves as a Process — its Create operation runs once, the moment the node is placed. Its Update operation runs every frame, automatically. Its Dispose operation runs when the node is removed.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This was available the whole time. We did not use it because Part V's use cases — bundling, dynamic instances, mutability comparisons — were about types as data being manipulated by hand. The Process behavior is the other mode of the same type. It is useful when you want one specific instance of a type to live in your patch and run by itself.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Lix9IGvpJf7PuuSfLL9TCj" Bounds="1044,23717,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Enabling Process Behavior">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="G03Xj8hllAHNWS8PMzGAqR" Bounds="1044,23759,405,467" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The setting is a single toggle in the Patch Explorer. Right-click your Record or Class definition, find &quot;Enable as Process&quot; in the menu, and turn it on. Nothing about the type changes — its properties stay, its operations stay, the existing places in your patch where you create instances by hand keep working.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;What changes is that you can now also place the type itself as a node, by typing its name in the Node Browser. The node has no input pin for State Input — it does not need one, it is the instance. Its Create runs when the node is placed. Its Update runs on every frame the patch is running. Its Dispose runs when the node is removed from the patch, when the patch closes, or when the application stops.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The reader who has worked through Part V can now recognize that this is exactly how Process nodes from chapter 11 have always worked. The &quot;Enable as Process&quot; toggle is the bridge that reveals it.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Lk4CbXGIABHO3kEtuIiPBp" Bounds="1514,23723,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Counter Example">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="DnF8rdSYgBHO7N25iMddDY" Bounds="1514,23765,405,339" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="In the patch on the right we have defined a small Record called Counter, with a single property — Count, an integer. It has an Update operation that increments Count by one each frame. With &quot;Enable as Process&quot; turned on, we place the Counter directly in the patch.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The Counter starts at zero on placement. Every frame, Update fires, incrementing Count. An IOBox connected to the Counter shows the count climbing. There is no spread, no pad, no manual call to Update — the type is running by itself.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is a singleton pattern at its simplest. One Counter, placed once, running for as long as the patch is open. If you want it to do more, you add more operations. If you want it to reset on a key press, you wire that in. The Counter is a Process node in everything but origin — it just happens to also be a Record, which means it could also be used as data elsewhere if you wanted.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="SskNh40lksyNFjWtq02Um6" Bounds="1514,24105,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=1092, y=820):**&#xD;&#xA;- A `Counter` Record (Process enabled) placed directly in the patch.&#xD;&#xA;- An IOBox showing the current Count value climbing.&#xD;&#xA;- A second IOBox showing &quot;frames since placement&quot; to make the per-frame Update behavior visible.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="J51VtqMwtcLPwr2PWZnvoV" Bounds="332,24314,277,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="ORDER OF OPERATIONS!">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="CCaFzd0MVF7LTGI7AJ5xcf" Bounds="1983,23735,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Dispose, Properly Demonstrated">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="IsVNrrHKHdoLU3MFpegl5T" Bounds="1983,23777,404,369" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="We mentioned Dispose in chapter 41 as the third reserved-color operation — the dark-red one — that runs when an instance is destroyed. Until now we did not have a clear, demonstrable moment for it to fire. With Process-enabled types, we do.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The patch on the right shows a Record called Logger, with a Create operation that prints &quot;Logger started&quot; to the console, an Update operation that does nothing visible, and a Dispose operation that prints &quot;Logger stopped.&quot; With Process enabled, we place the Logger in the patch and watch its messages appear.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;When the patch loads, Create fires and the console reads &quot;Logger started.&quot; Each frame, Update fires (silently, because we patched nothing into it). When you delete the Logger node from the patch — or when you close the patch entirely — Dispose fires, and the console reads &quot;Logger stopped.&quot;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is Dispose in its purest form. A clear moment for it to fire, a clear behavior to observe.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="TXKaVII7LNQLH6TlK6kJ1e" Bounds="1983,24166,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=1300):**&#xD;&#xA;- A `Logger` Record with Process enabled, with Create, Update, and Dispose operations defined.&#xD;&#xA;- Create logs &quot;Logger started&quot; via a Log or Print node.&#xD;&#xA;- Dispose logs &quot;Logger stopped.&quot;&#xD;&#xA;- An IOBox or console region showing the log output.&#xD;&#xA;- Instructions in an annotation IOBox: &quot;Delete the Logger node and watch the console.&quot;&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Qa9CbvKUL2mLMc5vY1w0a1" Bounds="2455,23731,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Two Reasons for Dispose to Fire">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="JzDTlRtGozPPI2LInT63Nx" Bounds="2455,23773,404,399" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A small detail worth knowing. Dispose fires for both Records and Classes when they are used as Process nodes — but for subtly different reasons.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;For a Class with Process enabled, Dispose fires when the instance is destroyed. The Class has identity. The runtime knows when the last reference to it goes away, and at that moment Dispose runs. This is the standard reference-based story you would expect from any language with reference types.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;For a Record with Process enabled, Dispose fires when the specific placement is removed. The Record itself, being a value, does not really get destroyed in the same way — but the runtime tracks the placement of the node, and when the placement goes away, the Record running there has its Dispose called.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The end result is the same — you can put logic in Dispose that runs when the type is no longer in the patch — but the underlying mechanism differs slightly. It is a tiny detail that becomes important only when you start wrapping resources that need careful cleanup, which is outside this tutorial's scope.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="IlMPJHMmwdOLD2A5nn3JCx" Bounds="2929,23732,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="When to Reach For It">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="N7LoSQ0S7NILSMmkapCkiB" Bounds="2929,23774,404,433" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="&quot;Enable as Process&quot; is for the place-and-forget case. When you want one specific instance of a type to exist in your patch and run autonomously, this is the mode for it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Singletons. A global audio engine that initializes once and runs for the life of the application. A debug overlay that draws each frame. A configuration manager that loads settings on Create and saves them on Dispose. These are all single, persistent instances — not collections that grow and shrink.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;When you do not want this mode is when you have many instances created and destroyed at runtime — the patterns from chapter 41. There, you want types used as data, with spreads and pads doing the management. Mixing the two — a Process-enabled type used in a spread — generally does not work the way you would expect, because the Process behavior assumes one specific placement, not many.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The decision is straightforward: one of these in your patch, or many of these in a spread? If one, Enable as Process. If many, leave it off and use the patterns from earlier in Part V.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="EdVPDYXJ7tINyO1Ae9zOLn" Bounds="3402,23733,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Family Revealed">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="S67bTcuvYDIMfuBFhCdZMI" Bounds="3402,23775,404,433" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Looking at all of this together, Process, Record, and Class come into focus as a single family of choices about how to define and use objects.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A Process node from chapter 11 is essentially a type with Process behavior enabled by default and no support for runtime instances. It is placed at edit time, runs by itself, and that is all.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A Record is the default object type. You can use it as data — passed through operations as values — or you can enable Process behavior and place it directly in your patch as a self-running unit. Both modes work; the type itself does not change.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A Class is the mutable counterpart. Same flexibility — usable as data via reference, or with Process behavior enabled — but with reference semantics and in-place modification.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The three names that seemed like three different things at the start of Part V are different settings on the same underlying machinery. The unity was always there. The chapter just made it visible.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="HrOklewM5BIQdJdTQtYUkO" Bounds="3887,23732,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Looking Back at Part V">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Opq9dN18qttP9t4dnK7w2s" Bounds="3887,23774,404,433" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Part V started with the recognition that you had been using objects since chapter 10 and defining them since chapter 11. It ends with the recognition that Process, Record, and Class are one family.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In between you learned to define your own data types from the ground up. You gave them properties and custom operations. You used them to bundle related data into clean spreads. You created and destroyed instances at runtime in response to user input. You met the mutability fork and learned to choose between Records and Classes deliberately. You met the collection question and learned to separate it from the object question.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;That is a lot. If you absorbed even half of it, you have moved from being a patcher who uses what vvvv provides to being one who extends vvvv with your own building blocks. The next part of the tutorial — once you continue — picks up from here and looks at how your applications begin to interact with everything outside themselves.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Overlay Id="OAfUJW5lkkXN0ISPDTdzLU" Bounds="4498,23797,383,298">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">10</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
